With the session time zone set to `Asia/Kolkata` (UTC+05:30), both columns receive the same literal:

```sql
CREATE TABLE ev (t timestamp, tz timestamptz);
INSERT INTO ev VALUES ('2024-05-17 10:00', '2024-05-17 10:00');
SET timezone = 'UTC';
SELECT t, tz FROM ev;
```

What is displayed?

Inside one transaction, three rows are inserted:

```sql
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (now());
SELECT pg_sleep(0.2);
INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (now());
INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (clock_timestamp());
COMMIT;

SELECT count(DISTINCT x) FROM t1;
```

What is the count?

A helper counts rows in a table chosen by the caller:

```sql
CREATE FUNCTION count_rows(tbl text) RETURNS bigint
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE n bigint;
BEGIN
  EXECUTE format('SELECT count(*) FROM %I', tbl) INTO n;
  RETURN n;
END $$;

SELECT count_rows('acct; DROP TABLE audit');
```

